<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Whalley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1607–c. 1675</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Whalley was an English Parliamentarian cavalry commander in the Civil War, a cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>, and one of the judges who signed the death warrant of Charles I in 1649. At the Restoration in 1660 he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> with his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-goffe">William Goffe</a>. Sheltered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a> in the New Haven Colony and later at Hadley, Massachusetts, they evaded royal agents, and he died in hiding.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-whalley.jpg" alt="Edward Whalley" width="260" height="96" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward Whalley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Signature_of_Edward_Whalley.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Whalley was the second son of Richard Whalley, High Sheriff of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottinghamshire">Nottinghamshire</a> in 1595, and his second wife Frances Cromwell, an aunt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>. He is said to have started out as a woollen-draper, farmed without success at Chadwell St. Mary in Essex in the 1620s and 1630s, and in 1639 fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> to escape his creditors. He married Judith Duffell of Rochester in 1626; their daughter Frances married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-goffe">William Goffe</a>. A second marriage, to Mary Middleton, gave him two more sons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Whalley began the war as a cornet in John Fiennes's troop, fought at the Battle of Edgehill and became major of Cromwell's regiment of horse. Cromwell praised his conduct at Gainsborough in 1643. He fought at the Battle of Marston Moor, commanded one of Cromwell's two cavalry regiments at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-naseby">Battle of Naseby</a>, and took Banbury. In 1647 he guarded Charles I at Hampton Court Palace and treated him so courteously that the king later thanked him by letter. He was the fourth to sign the death warrant, immediately after Cromwell.</p><p>Soldiers of his regiment took part in the Bishopsgate Mutiny of April 1649. Whalley was wounded at the Battle of Dunbar, fought at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a>, sat for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottinghamshire">Nottinghamshire</a> in the parliaments of 1654 and 1656 and served as one of the major-generals, over Lincoln, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottingham">Nottingham</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a>, Warwick and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>. He joined the new House of Lords in December 1657. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>'s death he backed Richard Cromwell in vain and was dismissed from his command.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Whalley and Goffe landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> on 27 July 1660 and at first lived openly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>. They left on 26 February 1661, shortly before orders for their arrest arrived, and in New Haven were sheltered by the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-davenport">John Davenport</a>, hiding at times in a cave on West Rock now called Judges Cave. From October 1664 they lived with the minister John Russell at Hadley. Whalley was alive but in poor health in 1674 and probably died soon after. Streets in New Haven and Hadley bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edward Whalley” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1661 the colony sheltered the regicides <b>Edward Whalley</b> and William Goffe from royal agents.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-haven-colony">New Haven Colony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Hadley, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53,-1&amp;year=1651" aria-label="England on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4528 338 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Edward Whalley"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4978 608h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4978 608h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53,-1&amp;year=1651">What was here in 1651? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
